S = {
"Accelerate": "Nicole Forsgren, Jez Humble, and Gene Kim report four years of State of DevOps survey research and the four metrics that separate high performers from the rest: deployment frequency, lead time for changes, change failure rate, and time to restore. The argument is that software delivery performance predicts organizational performance, and that culture, architecture, and continuous delivery practices are what drive it.",
"Elon Musk": "Walter Isaacson shadowed Musk for two years and interviewed everyone around him for this biography, from a brutal South African childhood through PayPal, Tesla, SpaceX, Neuralink, and the Twitter purchase. It is a portrait of risk tolerance and cruelty in the same person, and of how much of the drive comes from the same place as the damage.",
"Algorithms to Live By": "Brian Christian and Tom Griffiths take problems computer science has solved, like when to stop searching, how to schedule, how to cache, and when to explore versus exploit, and show how the same math applies to apartments, closets, and calendars. The payoff is permission: some problems are hard for everyone, and the optimal strategy still gets it wrong a known share of the time.",
"Ask Your Developer": "Twilio founder Jeff Lawson argues that every company is now a software company and that the winners treat developers as creative partners rather than order-takers. Small autonomous teams, clear customer problems, and the discipline to build what differentiates you and buy the rest. A CEO's book about why the people closest to the code should be in the room.",
"Empowered": "Marty Cagan and Chris Jones on what it takes to run product teams that are given problems to solve rather than features to build: coaching, staffing, product vision and strategy, team objectives, and the leadership behaviors that make or break autonomy. The sequel to Inspired, aimed at the managers rather than the product people.",
"Man's Search for Meaning": "Viktor Frankl's account of surviving Auschwitz and Dachau, followed by the outline of logotherapy, the psychology he built from it. The thesis is that people can endure almost anything if they find meaning in it, and that the last human freedom is choosing one's attitude in a given set of circumstances. Short, and one of the most reread books of the twentieth century.",
"Nonviolent Communication": "Marshall Rosenberg's method for saying hard things without attacking: observe without judging, name the feeling, name the need behind it, and make a clear request. The claim is that most conflict is unmet needs expressed as blame, and that the four steps work in marriages, boardrooms, and classrooms alike. Earnest, sometimes awkward, and widely used.",
"The Body Keeps the Score": "Bessel van der Kolk's synthesis of three decades of trauma research: how overwhelming experience rewires the brain and the body, why talk alone often fails, and what helps, from EMDR and yoga to neurofeedback and theater. The book that moved trauma from a psychiatric niche into general conversation, and the source of the idea that feelings are information rather than instructions.",
"Think Like a Rocket Scientist": "Ozan Varol, a former Mars rover engineer turned law professor, distills nine strategies from aerospace into everyday thinking: reason from first principles, run thought experiments, test as you fly, and treat failure and success as equally suspect. Light on rocket science, heavy on how to question defaults.",
"Shadow Cell": "Andrew Bustamante's memoir of his years as a CIA covert operative, written with his wife and fellow officer Jihi Bustamante. Recruitment, training at the Farm, operations overseas, and the slow toll of living two lives, ending with the decision to leave. A spy memoir that spends as much time on marriage and trust as on tradecraft.",
"The Decision": "Kevin Hart's Audible Original, part memoir and part motivational talk, framed around the idea that life is a series of decisions and that the big ones get made in small, repeated choices. Comedy as a career case study: failed sets, a flop sitcom, and the work ethic that turned both into material.",
"Forever Strong™": "Dr. Gabrielle Lyon's case for muscle as the organ of longevity: protein targets most people miss, resistance training as medicine, and a reframing of obesity as under-muscled rather than over-fat. Practical plans for food and lifting, with the argument that healthy aging starts with skeletal muscle decades before it looks like a problem.",
"Greenlights": "Matthew McConaughey's memoir, built from thirty-five years of diaries, about the moments life said yes, including the ones that looked like no at the time. Rom-com purgatory, the pivot to Dallas Buyers Club, a year in a trailer, his father's death. Read by the author, which is most of the appeal.",
"Secrets to Winning at Office Politics": "Marie McIntyre's guide to the part of work nobody admits to: how influence actually flows, how to read power without becoming a schemer, and how to stop sabotaging yourself in meetings. Framed as political intelligence for people who find the whole subject distasteful but keep losing to those who don't.",
"Solve for Happy": "Mo Gawdat, then chief business officer at Google X, lost his son Ali to a routine surgery and wrote this book from the engineering model of happiness he had built years earlier: happiness is greater than or equal to your perception of events minus your expectations of how life should be. Six illusions to drop, seven blind spots to correct, five truths to hold.",
"Starry Messenger": "Neil deGrasse Tyson applies a cosmic perspective to earthbound arguments: politics, race, gender, war, law, and what we eat. The thread is that science is a way of knowing that cuts through tribal certainty, and that seeing Earth from far enough away changes what seems worth fighting over. Essays, not astrophysics.",
"The Art of Raising a Puppy": "The Monks of New Skete, who have bred and trained German shepherds at their monastery for decades, on the first year of a dog's life: choosing a breeder, the critical socialization weeks, crate training, leadership without force, and the relationship the training is really building. The standard text for people who want a dog, not a project.",
"This Is Your Brain on Food": "Dr. Uma Naidoo, a Harvard psychiatrist and trained chef, on the gut-brain connection and how food affects anxiety, depression, ADHD, sleep, and memory. Condition by condition, what the evidence says to eat and avoid, with recipes. Nutritional psychiatry for a general reader.",
}
